LED switch
The first project we cover combine a tactile switch (push button) and an LED. We want the LED to be turned on when the switch is pressed, and off otherwise. How would we do this? Think about it for a little while.
The code below illustrates a simple approach to do that. The first part consists in configuring the GPIOs we need, one as an output to control the LED, and one as an input, to read the state of the button. We also configure the button input with a pull-down resistor, internal to the Pico, so that it should work even without the 10kΩ on the diagram.
Then the key part of the code consists in running an infinite loop that tests the state of the button and update the state of the LED accordingly. Read it, understand it, and try it on your Pico.
What would you change to invert the role of the button: LED off when pushed, but on otherwise?

CMicroPython


#include "pico/stdlib.h"

#define LED_PIN    15
#define BUTTON_PIN 16

int main() {
    stdio_init_all();

    // Configure LED pin as digital output
    gpio_init(LED_PIN);
    gpio_set_dir(LED_PIN, GPIO_OUT);

    // Configure Button pin as digital input
    gpio_init(BUTTON_PIN);
    gpio_set_dir(BUTTON_PIN, GPIO_IN);

    // Enable internal pull-down resistor so GP16 reads LOW when the button is open
    gpio_pull_down(BUTTON_PIN);

    while (true) {
        // Read the button state (returns true if HIGH/VCC, false if LOW)
        bool state = gpio_get(BUTTON_PIN);

        // Turn LED on if pressed, off if released
        gpio_put(LED_PIN, state);

        // Brief delay to give the CPU a tiny rest
        sleep_ms(10);
    }
}


