Dealing with the debouncing with a timer
A timer interrupt is an event that is triggered automatically by a microcontroller’s internal hardware clock after a precise duration or at fixed time intervals. Rather than relying on blocking delays like sleep_ms(), which freeze CPU execution, a hardware timer, located within the microcontroller chip, counts clock cycles in the background. Once the counter reaches a target value, the hardware automatically generates an interrupt, pausing the main loop to execute a designated callback function before resuming normal operation (as for GPIO interrupts). There are two type of timer interrupts: “one-shot” alarms for deferred actions (like debouncing a button press in our example) or periodic tickers for precise, recurring tasks (like reading a sensor at exactly 100 Hz). Timer interrupts allow C and MicroPython programs to handle precise time-critical operations concurrently without wasting CPU cycles.
In our example, to tackle the debouncing, we want to only toggle the LED for the first transition from low to high on the pin, and ignore the bounces for a short while after this. To do this, the idea is the following: when the button is pressed, the interrupt deactivate itself, toggles the LED, and launches a timer for a few hundred milliseconds after which a callback function is triggered to reinstate the pin interrupt, ready for the next press of the button. Thecode below does this for us. Study the code, and try it.
You will notice that the led_state variables are not used anymore. What did we change to enable this?

CMicroPython


In this example in C, there are two different functions used to configure the GPIO interrupt: gpio_set_irq_enabled_with_callback and gpio_set_irq_enabled. The former sets the interrupt and attach the callback function, but the other simply active or deactivate it. This is because the Pico SDK uses interrupts in a way that is slightly unusual. There is only one callback function for all interrupts, and the callback function has to decide, based on the value of the pin and event, what to do. In other microcontrollers, it is often the case that each event is attached to its owned callback function directly.
The documentation of the SDK can be helpful to better understand these functions: SDK - IRQ functions.
#include "pico/stdlib.h"

#define LED_PIN          15
#define BUTTON_PIN       16
#define DEBOUNCE_MS     300   // Adjust debounce duration here (in milliseconds)

// Forward declaration of the timer callback
int64_t timer_callback(alarm_id_t id, void *user_data);

// GPIO Interrupt Callback (Fires on button press)
void gpio_callback(uint gpio, uint32_t events) {
    if (gpio == BUTTON_PIN) {
        // Step A: Disable button interrupt during debounce period
        gpio_set_irq_enabled(BUTTON_PIN, GPIO_IRQ_EDGE_RISE, false);

        // Step B: Read current pin state, invert it, and update the LED
        gpio_put(LED_PIN, !gpio_get(LED_PIN));

        // Step C: Start single-shot timer to re-enable the interrupt
        add_alarm_in_ms(DEBOUNCE_MS, timer_callback, NULL, false);
    }
}

// Timer Callback (Fires after DEBOUNCE_MS elapses)
int64_t timer_callback(alarm_id_t id, void *user_data) {
    // Re-enable the button interrupt after bounce has settled
    gpio_set_irq_enabled(BUTTON_PIN, GPIO_IRQ_EDGE_RISE, true);

    // Return 0 so the alarm does not repeat
    return 0;
}

int main() {
    stdio_init_all();

    // Configure LED pin
    gpio_init(LED_PIN);
    gpio_set_dir(LED_PIN, GPIO_OUT);

    // Configure Button pin
    gpio_init(BUTTON_PIN);
    gpio_set_dir(BUTTON_PIN, GPIO_IN);
    gpio_pull_down(BUTTON_PIN);

